Container Image Identity and Tags
Objectives Relate container image tags to their identifier hashes, 
and identify container images from pods and containers on Kubernetes nodes.

Container Image Structure
A full image name has several parts:
registry.redhat.io/rhel9/nginx-124:9.6
Component	Example Value	Purpose
Registry server	registry.redhat.io	Where the image is stored
Namespace	rhel9	Logical grouping of images
Name	nginx-124	Image software name (may include version)
Tag	9.6	Points to a specific version of the image
•	If the tag is omitted, latest is used by default.
________________________________________
Tags: Floating vs Static
•	Floating Tags: e.g., latest, 1
o	Can point to different versions over time.
o	New pods may pull a newer version unintentionally.
•	Static Tags: e.g., 9.6
o	Guaranteed not to change.
o	Ensures application consistency.
________________________________________
SHA Image IDs
•	Each image has a unique SHA-256 digest.
•	Referring by SHA ensures the image is immutable:
registry.redhat.io/rhel9/nginx-124@sha256:126903359331...
•	Retrieve SHA ID from a tag:
oc image info --filter-by-os linux/amd64 registry.redhat.io/rhel9/nginx-124:9.6
•	On nodes, list images with their SHA IDs:
crictl images --digests --no-trunc
________________________________________
Image Pull Policy
Controls how OpenShift pulls images to nodes:
Policy	Behavior
IfNotPresent	Uses local image if available; pulls otherwise. (default for tags or SHA)
Always	Always checks registry for updates. (default for latest tag)
Never	Expects image already on node; fails if not present.
________________________________________
Garbage Collection of Images
•	Images are not automatically deleted when pods are removed.
•	Kubelet garbage collector runs every 5 minutes:
o	Removes oldest unused images if disk >85% full.
o	Stops when usage <80%.
•	Check image storage:
crictl imagefsinfo
•	Manual removal (less recommended):
crictl rmi <image>
________________________________________
Best Practices
1.	Prefer static tags or SHA IDs for consistent deployments.
2.	Use IfNotPresent for stable images, Always for dynamic tags (latest).
3.	Rely on the garbage collector to manage node disk usage.
4.	Be aware of multi-architecture images and use --filter-by-os when inspecting.


